<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portobelo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Porto Bello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portobelo is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of Panama, founded in 1597 to replace Nombre de Dios as the Atlantic end of the treasure route across the isthmus. Its fairs, held when the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> arrived, exchanged Peruvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for European goods, and its forts were sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a> in 1668 and taken by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-vernon">Edward Vernon</a> in 1739. With nearby San Lorenzo, its fortifications are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portobelo.jpg" alt="Portobelo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tedder · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portobelo_Ruins_and_bay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
